arguments.content:
🚨 **BLOCKING: CI red — `cleanupEmptySessions` broke the CRAP ratchet (13.00 > 12.0)**

The `CRAP + duplication` quality gate fails on this exact head (`4c1ffbfd`, run 44595104632):

```
13.00  100.0  N/A   13  JsonlSessionRepo.cleanupEmptySessions   lib/src/session/session_repo.dart:616
Max CRAP: 13.00 — FAIL (threshold: 12.00)
```

The two #2320 guards added here — the parts guard at `session_repo.dart:648` (+1) and the named guard at `:655` (`id != null && ...`, +2) — push the method from CC 12 (the ratchet ceiling `main` already sits at, `crap4dart.yaml:51`) to CC 13. With 100% coverage, CRAP = CC, so the gate cannot pass as written. Per the repo's own doctrine the threshold only moves down ("Only down from here", `crap4dart.yaml:34`) and the gh-1738 baseline mechanism is for legacy debt, not a fresh regression introduced by this PR — so the fix is to bring the method back under 12.

Suggested shape: extract the per-entry exemption decision into a private predicate — the loop body keeps only the read/emptiness logic:

```dart
bool _sweepEntryExempt({
  required FileInfo entry,
  required String path,
  required Set<String> live,
  required Set<String> namedIds,
  required Set<String> partPrimaries,
  required String? exemptSessionId,
  required DateTime? cutoff,
}) {
  if (partPrimaries.contains(_sessionPathKey(path))) return true;
  final id = _sessionIdFromPath(path);
  if (id != null && live.contains(id)) return true;
  if (id == exemptSessionId) return true;
  if (id != null && namedIds.contains(id)) return true;
  return cutoff != null &&
      !DateTime.fromMillisecondsSinceEpoch(entry.mtimeMs).isBefore(cutoff);
}
```

That splits the branch chain across two methods, leaving both well under 12 while behavior is byte-identical — and the five suites pinning this method (`session_deletion_test`, `header_only_session_sweep_test`, `session_repo_test`, `session_listing_index_test`, plus the #2320 pins) already cover it 100%, so the refactor is mechanically safe. Note the guards are the *right* guards — this is purely a gate-arithmetic problem, no behavioral change is requested.

